PRISM — Production Improvement Roadmap
🔴 1. GitHub Authentication & Security
- Proper GitHub OAuth / preferably GitHub App for the product
- Securely store GitHub access tokens — encrypt at rest
- Never expose GitHub client secret to frontend
- Handle expired/revoked GitHub tokens
- Handle OAuth failures gracefully
- Secure session management
- Proper CSRF/state validation
- Secure cookie configuration
- GitHub permission/scope minimization
🔴 2. GitHub API Layer
- Create a dedicated GitHub API service
- Fetch PR details reliably
- Fetch changed files
- Handle GitHub pagination
- Handle 401/403/404 errors
- Handle GitHub rate limits
- Handle API timeouts/retries
- Avoid duplicating GitHub API logic across controllers
🔴 3. Webhooks
Eventually PRISM shouldn't depend on the user manually starting every review.
- GitHub webhook integration
- Verify webhook using HMAC SHA-256
- Handle pull_request events
- Detect opened/updated/reopened PRs
- Prevent duplicate webhook processing
- Store webhook/review event information
🔴 4. Background Processing / Queue
This is important for real-world usage.
Instead of:
User request
   ↓
Fetch PR
   ↓
OpenAI
   ↓
Wait...
we want:
GitHub webhook
   ↓
Create review job
   ↓
Queue
   ↓
Worker
   ↓
AI review
- Redis
- Job queue
- Background worker
- Job status (QUEUED, PROCESSING, COMPLETED, FAILED)
- Retry failed jobs
- Prevent duplicate jobs
- Handle worker crashes
Your existing ReviewStatus enum is already a good foundation for this.
🔴 5. Large PR Handling
Your current pipeline needs to become much more robust here.
- Ignore generated/unnecessary files
- Ignore lock files where appropriate
- Handle very large diffs
- Token budget management
- Split large PRs into chunks
- Review chunks independently
- Combine findings
- Avoid sending the same code/context repeatedly
🔴 6. AI Review Reliability
Your OpenAI + Zod structured output is already a good foundation. We improve around it rather than replacing it.
- Handle OpenAI API failures
- Retry transient failures
- Handle malformed/unexpected responses
- Validate every AI response with Zod
- Token/cost tracking
- Timeout handling
- Model configuration instead of hardcoding
- Better review prompts
- Consistent severity/category definitions
- Prevent hallucinated file/line references where possible
🔴 7. Finding Management
This is one of the biggest upgrades toward a real CodeRabbit-like experience.
Currently:
AI → findings → database
Eventually:
Finding
 ├── file
 ├── line
 ├── severity
 ├── message
 ├── suggestion
 ├── commit SHA
 ├── fingerprint
 └── status
Then:
- Finding fingerprints
- Duplicate detection
- Track findings across commits
- Mark findings resolved
- Mark findings outdated
- Avoid repeating the same comment
- Track which review generated the finding
The gh-review-loop repo is particularly useful for ideas here.
🔴 8. GitHub Review Comments
This is one of the major PRISM milestones.
- Create GitHub PR review
- Post summary review
- Post inline comments
- Map AI finding → GitHub file/line
- Store GitHub comment/review IDs
- Update/remove stale comments
- Avoid comment spam
- Handle GitHub comment API failures
🟠 9. Database Improvements
Your Prisma/PostgreSQL foundation is already good.
We should improve:
- Proper indexes
- Finding status
- Commit SHA
- GitHub review/comment IDs
- Review/job metadata
- Token encryption
- Database transactions where needed
- Cleanup/retention strategy
- Migration strategy
🟠 10. Error Handling
Right now we have several places where you're doing:
throw new Error(...)
That's fine during development, but production needs more structured handling.
- Centralized error handling
- User-friendly errors
- Safe errors — never expose secrets
- GitHub errors separated from AI errors
- Database errors handled
- Retryable vs non-retryable errors
- Error logging
- Review failure states
🟠 11. Security Hardening
- Validate all external input
- Secure OAuth flow
- HMAC webhook verification
- Token encryption
- Rate limiting
- Secure cookies
- Security headers
- Least-privilege GitHub permissions
- Never log access tokens/API keys
- Protect internal endpoints
- Validate GitHub webhook payloads
🟠 12. Testing
A real product needs confidence that changes don't break the review pipeline.
- OAuth tests
- Session tests
- GitHub API tests
- File filtering tests
- Prompt builder tests
- Zod/schema tests
- Review processor tests
- Finding deduplication tests
- Webhook tests
- End-to-end review test
Especially:
GitHub PR
 ↓
Webhook
 ↓
Queue
 ↓
Worker
 ↓
AI
 ↓
Database
 ↓
GitHub comment
We eventually want at least one end-to-end test covering that flow.
🟡 13. Observability
When a real user says:
"PRISM didn't review my PR."

you need to know why.
- Structured logging
- Review/job IDs
- Processing duration
- GitHub API latency/errors
- OpenAI latency/errors
- Token usage
- Review failure reasons
- Health check endpoint
- Monitoring/error tracking
🟡 14. Deployment & Infrastructure
- Production environment variables
- Proper secret management
- Docker
- PostgreSQL production setup
- Redis production setup
- Worker deployment
- HTTPS
- Database migrations
- CI/CD
- Production logging
